sysforge(1)

; Hand-written scdoc template. The COMMANDS section below is generated by
; tools/gen_options.py from the argparse tree — edit prose here, edit option
; help text in sysforge/cli.py, then run `make man`.

# NAME

sysforge - build and maintenance suite and system bootstrapper for Arch Linux

# SYNOPSIS

*sysforge* [*-v*...] _COMMAND_ [_OPTIONS_] [_ARGS_...]

# DESCRIPTION

SysForge builds AUR and custom packages with rule-matched compiler-flag
profiles: every package is built with the optimization flags its matching
profile resolves (-march=native, LTO, linker choice, caches), while pacman
remains the owner of the package database. Build metadata — versions, the
resolved flags, toolchain identity, source provenance, and the reviewed
source commit — is recorded per package in build_state.toml and drives
update, drift detection, and diagnostics.

*sysforge update* is the steady-state entry point: it walks the live install
set (all foreign packages, plus repo packages selected by overrides or
repo_mode = "build_from_source"), syncs sources through a rate-limited scheduler,
compares versions, reports toolchain-variant and compiler-flag drift, and
rebuilds what changed. Before any changed source is built, the PKGBUILD
review gate presents the full source-tree diff since the last accepted
build for approval (see *--no-review*). Builds run through a managed
makepkg invocation with dependencies pre-installed, per-package logs, build
failure diagnosis with deterministic auto-repair for known failure modes,
and a single bulk install at the end.

*sysforge run* executes pipeline stages: the bootstrap stages that take a
fresh Arch ISO environment to a configured system (install via archinstall,
hardware detection, configure), and the repeatable stages (toolchain,
kernel, packages, reconfigure) that build an optionally PGO-optimized LLVM
toolchain and a custom kernel under boot-safety gates.

*sysforge doctor* is the read-only health surface, split into two scopes.
*doctor system* runs the system-state axes (hardware, toolchain, pacman db,
state, boot, storage, services, graphics); bare *sysforge doctor* is that
sweep. *doctor pkg* runs the package-scoped axes over one or more targets:
*--abi* (depends + ABI linkage), *--rust* (rust-toolchain.toml pins), and
*--integrity* (pacman -Qkk). With no axis flag each scope runs its defaults;
a broad target selector (*--all*, *--repo*) suppresses the opt-in axes unless
named explicitly.

The flat *doctor* flags were removed in 3.0.0. Each one now prints its
replacement and exits 2; that hint table is itself removed in 3.1.0.

[[ *removed*
:< *replacement*
|  _--boot_, _--hardware_, _--toolchain_, _--cache_, _--pacman_, _--state_, _--restart_, _--storage_, _--services_, _--audio_, _--network_, _--distro_, _--gfxperf_
:< *sysforge doctor system* _<same flag>_
|  _--all_
:< *sysforge doctor system* then *sysforge doctor pkg --all*
|  _--repo_
:< *sysforge doctor pkg --repo*
|  _--graphics_
:< *sysforge doctor system --graphics* (probes) or *sysforge doctor pkg --graphics* (targets)
|  _--rust_
:< *sysforge doctor system --rust* (effective toolchain) or *sysforge doctor pkg* _PKG_ *--rust* (pin)
|  _--integrity_
:< *sysforge doctor system --integrity* or *sysforge doctor pkg* _PKG_ *--integrity*
|  _PKG_ (bare positional)
:< *sysforge doctor pkg* _PKG_

Install-bearing commands write a stage sentinel before mutating the system;
an interrupted run is detected on the next invocation and offers a recorded
recovery command.

# GLOBAL OPTIONS

*-v*, *--verbose*
	Increase verbosity. Default shows errors only; -v adds warnings, -vv
	informational messages, -vvv debug output (repeatable, position
	independent). The default level (when no flag is passed) can be raised
	via the *[log] verbosity* key in _sysforge.toml_; a CLI flag always wins.

*--quiet*
	Suppress all non-error output (verbosity 0), overriding both *-v* and
	the *[log] verbosity* config default. File logs still record every
	level. Distinct from *doctor*'s local *--quiet* (which suppresses clean
	diagnostic lines).

*-V*, *--version*
	Print the sysforge version and exit.

*--py-profile*
	Run the verb under the Python profiler (cProfile) and print the top
	functions by cumulative time to stderr at exit. Position independent.
	Profiles sysforge's own runtime only; subprocess work (makepkg, pacman,
	git) appears as wait time — use *--timings* for wall-clock phase costs.

*--py-profile-out* _FILE_
	Write raw cProfile stats to _FILE_ for later analysis (pstats,
	snakeviz). Implies *--py-profile*.

*--timings*
	Print a wall-clock phase timing report (source sync, version check,
	drift detection, per-package builds, install, pacman -Syu) after
	*build* and *update* runs. Without the flag the report is still
	written to the log at info level.

*--color* _auto_|_always_|_never_
	Colorize output. _auto_ (the default) colours when writing to a
	terminal and honours the *NO_COLOR* and *FORCE_COLOR* environment
	variables; _always_ forces colour on (useful when piping into a pager
	such as *less -R*); _never_ disables it. Overrides the *[ui] color*
	key in _sysforge.toml_.

*--no-throttle*
	Ignore the configured build throttle (*nice*/*ionice*/*cpu_quota*/*jobs*
	from *[build]*) for this run, building at normal unthrottled priority.

*--turbo*
	Run the build at _higher_ than default priority: negative niceness and
	best-effort IO, with no CPU ceiling or job cap. Stronger than
	*--no-throttle*. Lowering niceness may require privilege; when the kernel
	refuses, the build runs at the current priority (best-effort, never fatal).

# COMMANDS

@OPTIONS@

# FILES

/etc/sysforge/profiles.toml
	Flag profiles, match rules, conflict groups, and paths
	(pkgbuild_src_dir). Overridable per run with *--profile-conf*.
	Read by: *build*, *fetch*, *update*, *resolve*, *doctor*, *env*,
	and the *run* stages.

/etc/sysforge/packages.toml
	Package manifest: build-rule overrides applied to the live install
	set, the [build] table (repo_mode, review, pkgbuild_src_dir), and the
	bootstrap install list. Managed by *sysforge packages*.
	Read by: *build*, *update*, *run packages*, *run pipeline*,
	*run reconfigure*.

/etc/sysforge/sysforge.toml
	General settings: UI, git, failure_handling (auto-repair behaviour
	per scenario), safety.
	Read by: *build*, *fetch*, *update* (git/clone settings),
	*run toolchain*, *run pipeline*, *run reconfigure*.

/etc/sysforge/toolchain.toml, /etc/sysforge/kernel.toml
	Opt-in toolchain and kernel stage configuration (enabled = false by
	default in both). kernel.toml *kconfig_targets* (default: unset)
	names an explicit *make* kconfig-generation sequence, replacing the
	PKGBUILD's own kconfig steps (the sysforge.config fragment merge
	still applies first): silent targets
	(olddefconfig, defconfig, allmodconfig, alldefconfig, savedefconfig,
	listnewconfig) always run; prompting targets (oldconfig,
	localmodconfig, localyesconfig, mod2yesconfig) require an
	interactive stage run and are rejected otherwise (oldconfig names
	its silent equivalent olddefconfig); at most one UI target (config,
	nconfig, menuconfig, xconfig, gconfig) is allowed and it always
	runs last; randconfig is never allowed. localmodconfig/
	localyesconfig are warned against as high-risk/low-reward — they
	over-minimize to modules currently loaded and accumulate the lsmod
	snapshot at <state_dir>/lsmod.snapshot (delete it to reset).
	Read by: *run toolchain* / *run kernel*, *run pipeline*,
	*run reconfigure*, and *doctor* on the matching axes.

build_state.toml (under the state directory)
	Per-package build records (a superset of pacman -Q) plus the table
	of recorded build failures. Inspect with *sysforge state*; the state
	directory resolves from *--state-dir*, $SYSFORGE_STATE_DIR, or the
	default /var/lib/sysforge.
	Read by: *state* (read/write), *build*, *update*, *doctor*, and the
	install-bearing *run* stages.

# ENVIRONMENT

SYSFORGE_STATE_DIR
	Override the state directory (pipeline state, build_state.toml,
	logs, sentinels). Read by every command that touches build state:
	*build*, *update*, *state*, *log*, *doctor*, and the *run* stages.

SYSFORGE_CONFIG_DIR
	Override the configuration directory searched before
	/etc/sysforge. Read by every command that loads a config file (see
	FILES).

SYSFORGE_EDITOR
	Editor for the interactive config review in *run reconfigure*.
	Falls back to $EDITOR, then $VISUAL.

PAGER
	Pager for *sysforge log*, *state* listings, and PKGBUILD review
	diffs in *build*/*update* (falls back to less -RFX, then more).

# DISTRIBUTIONS

SysForge assumes *pacman*(8) and *makepkg*(8), and nothing narrower. It has no
distro-conditional behaviour: distro identity is reported, never branched on.

Arch Linux
	The primary supported base. Everything is validated here, including
	bootstrap/install, kernel staging, and the graphics/DKMS probes.

CachyOS
	A validated derivative, re-checked each minor release for packaging
	invariants, dependency resolution, *makepkg.conf*(5) handling, and
	version comparison. The bootstrap, kernel-staging and graphics/DKMS
	features are *not* validated here.

Other Arch derivatives
	Expected to work but unvalidated. SysForge hardcodes no sync-repo
	names and never replaces the system *makepkg.conf*(5), so a derivative's
	own repos and toolchain defaults are honoured.

Run *sysforge doctor system --distro* to see which tier the running system falls in.
Anything not Arch-derived is out of scope.

# EXIT STATUS

0 on success. 1 on build/install/verification failure. 2 when the user
declines a gate (stale-sentinel recovery refused, PKGBUILD review aborted).

# EXAMPLES

Build one package with its matched profile:

	sysforge build htop

Update every sysforge-managed package, resolving VCS pkgver() up front:

	sysforge update --devel

Read-only drift report (no network, no rebuild):

	sysforge update --offline --dry-run

Inspect which profile a PKGBUILD would get and why:

	sysforge resolve mesa-git --show-flags

Full system health sweep:

	sysforge doctor

# SEE ALSO

*makepkg*(8), *pacman*(8), *PKGBUILD*(5), *makepkg.conf*(5)

